# kontra — local Compose cluster knobs (ADR 0047).
#
# OPTIONAL. `docker-compose.yml` runs with nothing beside it; every `${…:-default}` in it resolves to
# a published image and a working port. Fetch this file only when you want to change one:
#
#   curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/medmahmoudi26/kontra/dev/.env.quickstart
#
# It is named `.env.quickstart` rather than `.env` because a repository cannot ship a tracked `.env`
# that a developer's own ignored `.env` does not then shadow. The `-o .env` is what makes it the one
# Compose reads.
#
# THE FOUR THINGS PEOPLE ACTUALLY COME HERE FOR: `KONTRA_BIND` (below, and read it), the port block,
# `KONTRA_EXPLORE_TOKEN` to turn on the query workbench and the logs rail, and `KONTRA_WORKSPACES` to
# put your code somewhere other than `./workspaces`.
#
# THIS IS NOT `.env.example`. That one is a development checkout. This file configures the
# Compose cluster: Postgres, Temporal, SeaweedFS, Redis, registry, orchestrator roles,
# named workspaces.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE `docker-compose.yml` SUBSTITUTES HAS TO APPEAR HERE, even if only in a comment.
# `cli/envexample_test.go:73` greps both files and fails on one this file does not mention — because
# a knob that exists in compose and is undocumented here is a knob nobody finds.

# ── the one line that matters for security ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# WHERE THE PORTS ARE PUBLISHED. `127.0.0.1` means only this machine can reach the control plane.
# A published Docker port bypasses ufw. Do not change this to 0.0.0.0.
KONTRA_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ── which images ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THESE ARE COMPOSE'S OWN DEFAULTS, WRITTEN OUT. Repeating them is the point of the file: you can see
# what you are about to change. Delete a line and you get the same value from `docker-compose.yml`.
#
# They used to be `kontra:latest` / `kontra-orchestrator:latest` / `kontra-host:1` with
# `KONTRA_PULL_POLICY=never` — local tags that only resolve on a machine that has already run
# `make image` plus three hand-typed `docker build`s. `never` was correct FOR THAT: it stopped Compose
# reaching for a registry that had nothing in it. With published images `never` is the opposite of
# what you want — it means Compose refuses to fetch what it does not already have, and the install
# fails on a machine that has never built anything, which is every new machine.
#
# `missing` pulls what is absent and leaves what is present alone. `docker compose pull` is how you
# take an update; nothing here pulls behind your back on every `up`.
#
# `:dev` FLOATS. It tracks the `dev` branch, which is where this file lives. A release pins every
# reference to a digest instead — `publish.yml` writes an `images.env` you append to this file, and
# those lines cannot be repointed by anybody.
KONTRA_IMAGE=ghcr.io/medmahmoudi26/kontra:dev
KONTRA_ORCHESTRATOR_IMAGE=ghcr.io/medmahmoudi26/kontra-orchestrator:dev
KONTRA_HOST_IMAGE=ghcr.io/medmahmoudi26/kontra-host:dev
# The log shipper and its parser, in an image rather than bind-mounted out of a checkout — which is
# one of the two things that let this install be a `curl` (control/images/Dockerfile.logship).
KONTRA_LOGSHIP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/medmahmoudi26/kontra-logship:dev
KONTRA_PULL_POLICY=missing

# ── where your actors and workflows live ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# BLANK means `./workspaces` — a directory beside `docker-compose.yml`, in whatever directory you ran
# `docker compose up` from. It is bind-mounted at the SAME absolute path inside every service that
# reads code, so a path the console or a tmux pane hands you is a path that exists on your host too.
#
# IT DOES NOT HAVE TO EXIST FIRST. The `cli` service runs `kontra workspace seed` on boot, so an
# empty (or absent) directory becomes a seeded one with `hello/` in it. That is why the install is two
# commands and not three.
#
# Each child (hello/, my-app/, …) is one workspace; `.current` picks the active one and the console's
# top-right selector writes that file. Set an absolute path only to keep your code somewhere else.
KONTRA_WORKSPACES=

# ── THE CHECKOUT, FOR `kontra deploy` ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# BLANK means `${PWD}` — the directory you ran `docker compose up` from. The `cli` service mounts it
# read-only AT THE SAME ABSOLUTE PATH and runs with it as `working_dir`, because that is what makes
# `docker exec kontra-cli kontra deploy --actor …` able to build: the mount alone was measured to be
# necessary and not sufficient, and the same command under the matching `working_dir` built and
# pushed. `:ro` because building reads the tree and nothing in that container should write to it.
#
# A pure-`curl` install has no checkout and needs none — it deploys published images. Set this only
# when you are running the Compose cluster against a clone that is NOT the directory you are in.
KONTRA_REPO=

# A Tenant IS a Temporal namespace.
KONTRA_NAMESPACE=default

# Host-side ports. Move one when something on this machine already holds it.
KONTRA_API_PORT=8088
KONTRA_TEMPORAL_PORT=7233
KONTRA_S3_PORT=8333
KONTRA_KV_PORT=6379
KONTRA_REGISTRY_PORT=5000
KONTRA_POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# The in-cluster registry as CLI and actors address it. Trust lists must name that host so
# unsigned local artifacts are placeable (ADR 0039). Compose defaults match these if you omit them.
KONTRA_REGISTRY=127.0.0.1:5000
KONTRA_TRUST_REGISTRIES=127.0.0.1:5000
KONTRA_TRUST_UNSIGNED=127.0.0.1:5000

# REGISTRY CREDENTIALS, AND EMPTY IS A CHOICE RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT.
#
# Blank means the registry accepts anonymous pulls and pushes, which is what `registry:2` did and
# what the loopback publish is the control for (the file header of docker-compose.yml). Fill all
# THREE to turn authentication on; the renderer refuses one or two, because a registry with auth
# enabled and no users answers 401 to every pull and reads as a broken install rather than a locked
# one. The roles are separate so a build that can push an actor still cannot replace a runtime.
#
# Under docker-compose.vpc.yml these stop being optional: that overlay publishes the registry to the
# fleet network, and the overlay demands them the same way it demands KONTRA_REDIS_PASSWORD.
KONTRA_REGISTRY_PUSH_ACTORS_PASSWORD=
KONTRA_REGISTRY_PUSH_RUNTIMES_PASSWORD=
KONTRA_REGISTRY_PULL_PASSWORD=

# zot's CVE scanning. Off because turning it on downloads a vulnerability database on first boot.
KONTRA_REGISTRY_CVE=

# Turns on the two surfaces that read a Run's CONTENT: the query workbench (`/api/datasets/query`)
# and the logs proxy (`/api/logs/*`). Both are fail-closed, so with this unset they answer 503 and
# name this variable rather than opening. Set it to any random string to enable them locally.
KONTRA_EXPLORE_TOKEN=

# How long VictoriaMetrics keeps a series, in months (ADR 0050 §1). Set explicitly rather than
# inherited: the default is one month, and the Fleet somebody asks about three months later is
# exactly the one whose series would have aged out silently. The backend itself is NOT published to
# the host — `kontra doctor` says whether it is reachable, and from the host the honest answer is
# "unpublished by design"; run `docker compose exec cli kontra doctor` to probe it for real.
KONTRA_METRICS_RETENTION=6

# The same, for logs. VictoriaLogs has NO AUTHENTICATION of its own — its multi-tenancy is a request
# header, so anything that can reach the port can read any tenant by changing a number. It is
# therefore not published to the host either, and the orchestrator will proxy it with the tenant
# stamped server-side (ADR 0050, issue #17). Do not "make it reachable" by publishing it.
KONTRA_LOGS_RETENTION=6

# THE LOGIN AND THE FOUR TOKENS ARE NOT SET HERE, and the five blank variables that used to sit
# at this spot are gone rather than left empty. Nothing read them: `docker-compose.yml` names none
# of the five in any `environment:` block, so a value typed here reached no container — a setting
# that looks like a knob and is not is worse than no knob at all.
#
# WHERE THEY ACTUALLY COME FROM: `kontra init` generates them on first boot and writes
# $KONTRA_HOME/runtime.env, which `control/images/orchestrator-entrypoint.sh` sources before
# starting the process. That is what puts KONTRA_RUN_TOKEN and KONTRA_PANEL_TOKEN in the API's
# environment, and it survives a recreate because the home is a named volume.
#
# The console login is generated on that same first boot and written to a 0600 file. It is NOT in
# the logs, and that is deliberate: `kontra init` runs inside the `cli` container, so anything it
# printed would live in `docker compose logs` for anyone in the docker group to read, beside a
# config.yaml that deliberately stores only an scrypt hash.
#
#   docker compose exec cli cat /var/lib/kontra/console-password
#
# Deleted that file? `docker compose exec cli kontra user add <name>` makes another account; the
# old password cannot be recovered, because only the hash is stored.
# Read a token? `docker compose exec cli grep KONTRA_RUN_TOKEN /var/lib/kontra/runtime.env`

# Pulumi encrypts dockerFleet state with this. Local-only default. `down -v` destroys that state
# AND every Dataset. Change it before you have a Fleet you care about; losing it makes stack
# secrets unreadable.
PULUMI_CONFIG_PASSPHRASE=local-dev

# Optional DigitalOcean fleet. Unused by docker_fleet().
DIGITALOCEAN_TOKEN=
KONTRA_CLOUD_CREDENTIAL=
KONTRA_PROBE_QUEUE=kontra-probe

# Pinned so `docker compose` finds the same stack whichever directory you run it from.
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=kontra

# DuckDB's memory budget for the API's lake reads. 512MB against ~1GB free on a small host.
# NOT an RSS cap — the cgroup is — so raising this above what the container can actually get
# trades a clean DuckDB error for the OOM killer taking the whole API with it.
KONTRA_DUCKDB_MEMORY_LIMIT=512MB

# Where the logs proxy reads from (ADR 0050 §1). In-compose and unpublished by default; a
# deployment with its own OpenTelemetry-compatible store re-points this one variable.
KONTRA_LOGS_URL=http://victorialogs:9428

# ── STORAGE RETENTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Whether the hourly sweep actually DELETES. Unset is a preview: it reports what it would collect
# and removes nothing. Set to 1/true/yes/on only after reading a preview
# (`GET /api/datasets/retention/preview`) — the first collecting run reaches the oldest, most
# appended-to output on the box.
KONTRA_RETENTION_COLLECT=

# How long a run's raw per-record unit blobs are kept, in days.
#
# This is the one that keeps the object store bounded: `units/` is one object per pushed record and
# is normally ~99% of the store by size. The control plane turns this into an S3 LIFECYCLE RULE on
# the `units/` prefix at boot, so the object store expires them itself — on SeaweedFS that means
# whole TTL volumes are unlinked, which is the only thing that actually returns disk (deleting
# objects one by one leaves holes that need a vacuum, and a vacuum needs free space to copy into).
#
# Do NOT set this below 1: expiry is day-granular, and values under a day are refused rather than
# floored to "expire everything now". The window is also what protects a materialization retry,
# which is bounded in hours — nothing checks the ledger before the store expires a blob.
KONTRA_UNITS_RETENTION_DAYS=7

# ── THE VPC ADDRESS, AND WHY IT IS NOT IN THIS STACK ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# UNUSED NOW, AND THAT IS THE POINT: every port publishes on `KONTRA_BIND` above, which is
# loopback. Nothing substitutes this name any more.
#
# (`docker-compose.quickstart.yml` is a SYMLINK to `docker-compose.yml` — one file, two names. So
# there is no second stack with a different posture, and there never was.)
#
# A cloud fleet does need more — a Droplet has to reach Temporal, Redis, the registry, S3 and the
# API to pull its Bundle and register, and loopback is not reachable from another host. That
# publish is an OPT-IN OVERLAY, not a default:
#
#   KONTRA_VPC_BIND=<this host's private address> \
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.vpc.yml up -d
#
# It has no default there on purpose. A private address is correct for exactly one host: wrong and
# the port fails to bind, empty and compose publishes with no host_ip — which is `0.0.0.0`, the
# whole control plane on every interface, arrived at by leaving a variable blank. The overlay
# spells it `${KONTRA_VPC_BIND:?…}` so an unset value refuses to start and says what to set.
#
# Leave it empty here. Setting it changes nothing in this file's stack.
KONTRA_VPC_BIND=

# ── THE STATE STORE'S PASSWORD, AND IT BELONGS TO THE SAME OVERLAY ────────────────────────────
#
# UNUSED BY THE QUICKSTART, for the same reason: Redis publishes on loopback here, and loopback is
# the control. The overlay publishes it to the fleet network so a Machine can reach it — and that
# store holds every actor's commit map and every global_state entry, so publishing it without a
# password hands a running Run's progress to anything on that VPC.
#
# The overlay spells it `${KONTRA_REDIS_PASSWORD:?...}`: no default, because a default password is
# the one an attacker tries first and Redis reads a BLANK requirepass as "no password" — which would
# make forgetting it indistinguishable from the exposure. Generate one per controller:
#
#   KONTRA_REDIS_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# Every client reads this name and sends AUTH only when it is set, so leaving it empty here changes
# nothing. The embedded appliance store has no password at all and says so if a client sends one.
KONTRA_REDIS_PASSWORD=

# ── PORTER: ARROW FLIGHT SQL OVER THE SAME DUCKLAKE ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A READ server, and it carries NO CREDENTIAL ON ITS FLIGHT PORT. The compose service therefore
# publishes no host port at all and is reachable only on the compose network, with the
# orchestrator as the authenticated front door — the posture `victorialogs` already has. A test
# (`compose.test.ts`) enforces the absent `ports:` entry, so this is not a comment anybody can
# quietly invalidate. Do not expose it, and do not send operator free-text SQL to it:
# `read_text('/etc/passwd')` and `read_csv('http://169.254.169.254/…')` are SELECTs, and
# `porter serve` has no filesystem or network control among its flags.
KONTRA_PORTER_URL=
KONTRA_PORTER_IMAGE=

# ── WHERE DUCKDB SPILLS WHEN A QUERY OUTGROWS ITS MEMORY LIMIT ────────────────────────────────
#
# A DEFAULT, BECAUSE THE ALTERNATIVE IS SILENTLY NOT SPILLING AT ALL. `SET temp_directory` is only
# issued when this resolves, so leaving it undefined does not mean "spill somewhere sensible" — it
# means DuckDB has nowhere to put anything and fails outright the moment a query needs more than
# `memory_limit`. Measured: campaign-1790599185 took six `Out of Memory Error` in ten minutes
# against a 1536MB limit while the container held 287 MiB of a 7.7 GiB allowance.
#
# Empty takes the compose default (/tmp/duckdb-spill).
KONTRA_DUCKDB_TEMP_DIR=

# ── THE FLEET SSH KEY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `KONTRA_SSH_KEY` is the path INSIDE the container, and the only one of the two this stack reads.
# `KONTRA_SSH_KEY_HOST` is the host file that has to appear beneath it, and the bind-mount that
# puts it there lives in `docker-compose.vpc.yml` with the rest of the fleet's reach — a quickstart
# that provisions no Droplets needs no key, and a key mounted by default is one developer's private
# half handed to every install. It must be the private half of a key DigitalOcean already has,
# because Droplets are created with `sshKeyIds` and only those keys can log in — see
# KONTRA_FLEET_SSH_KEY_IDS.
#
# THE WAY A MISSING KEY FAILS IS THE EXPENSIVE WAY: a machines-only converge needs no SSH and
# SUCCEEDS, so the Droplets come up; the placement converge then dies with ENOENT and `place()`
# raises with four Machines running and nothing on them. Measured on campaign-1790558895 —
# provisioned, billed, destroyed, having run nothing. Leave both empty for local-only work.
KONTRA_SSH_KEY=
KONTRA_SSH_KEY_HOST=
